# OS Unveiled
Context switch managed by hardware. It is very dependent on the hardware. As in, the hardware defines "context" and what it consists of. 

Rings describe user and kernel mode. Lower numbers is closer to kernel mode. Trap is an instruction that queries the trap handler table. 

There are privileged registers and a Current Privilege Level register.

The page table base register is called `CR3`. `CR2` is the address of the instruction accessing memory.

A page table entry has a present bit to indicate whether the page is in memory (`1`) or SWAP (`0`). If it's in SWAP, it points to where. If it's in SWAP, a page fault is sent for the hypervisor to handle. The physical host address may change. 



# Hypervisor
At some point, chip frequency hit a power wall that melted. Therefore, multi-core replaced faster chips.

Dark Silicon keeps part of the chip off to manage heat and not melt anything. This is facilitated with accelerators that specialize in certain tasks (e.g., an accelerator for encryption and decryption) that stay off when the task isn't needed at the moment. 

GPUs' high bandwidth memory (HBM) does have page tables that can map from VPA to HPA.

<!-- probably when the OS expects a certain GPU -->
The cycle is app, GPU instantion, GPU drivers, and the hardware GPU. Emulating the hardware is too difficult, so API remoting at the GPU instanution level is used. So when the VM makes a CUDA call, it is intercepted and transformed accordingly and sent to the GPU. This is slow but robust.



# Isolation
The 0-day attack targets a vulnerability that can gain access to a machine in less than a day.